The NetSurf libraries, as their authors released them.
======================================================

Five libraries from the NetSurf project: an HTML5 parser, a CSS parser and
selection engine, a DOM, and the two pieces they both stand on. Vendored for
the browser, and useful on their own.

Every one of them is MIT, checked against the COPYING each tarball ships
rather than against the website. The browser itself is GPLv2 and is NOT here.

    library          version   from download.netsurf-browser.org/libs/releases
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    libwapcaplet     0.4.3     string interning
    libparserutils   0.2.5     input streams and character sets
    libhubbub        0.3.8     the HTML5 parser
    libcss           0.9.2     the CSS parser and selection engine
    libdom           0.4.2     the DOM

Unmodified, byte for byte, and that is the rule rather than an aspiration:
`lua/upstream/` and `runtime/upstream/tinygl/` are here on the same terms.
Nothing in these trees has been patched to build on Kosmos, and nothing
needed to be - what it took was two libc functions (`bsearch`, `strtoul`),
one build switch that is their own (`-DWITHOUT_ICONV_FILTER`), and the
generated files below.

Generated, not shipped
----------------------

Four files these libraries build for themselves, and the build has to build
them too. They are generated into `build/gen-<variant>/netsurf/` rather than
beside the source, because output in a vendored tree is how a vendored tree
stops being what was released - which happened once here, by hand, and was
reverted.

    libparserutils   src/charset/aliases.inc            perl, from build/Aliases
    libhubbub        src/tokeniser/entities.inc         perl, from build/Entities
    libhubbub        treebuilder/autogenerated-*.c      gperf
    libcss           autogenerated_<property>.c  x119   their own gen_parser,
                                                        built with the HOST cc

The `build/` in `build/Aliases` and `build/Entities` is the library's own, and
it holds sources whatever the name suggests; libcss has one as well. That is
why `.gitignore` says `/build/` with its leading slash: without it all three
were ignored, the nine files in them were never committed, and a fresh clone
had nothing to generate these from.

`css_property_parser_gen.c` has a `main()` and is a build tool. It must never
be cross-compiled; the 119 property parsers it emits are what the target
needs. Missing them looks like 117 undefined `css__parse_*` symbols.

Not Lua
-------

These must be compiled *without* `-include lua/kosmos/kosmos_lua.h`, which
the ordinary user rule puts in front of everything. That header redirects
`time()` to the monotonic counter for `lmathlib`'s benefit, and these
libraries want the wall clock.
